# vanishBlockDisplays()
#
# The colour going in. Shrinks each display to nothing once it has
# settled onto the child, walking iro0..iro34 at the same stepTicks
# cadence moveBlockDisplays dispatched them on — so if this is started
# interpolationTicks after the final pass began, every display winks out
# on the exact tick it finishes its flight, in the order they arrive.
#
# Only the final pass should be followed by this. The driver fires it on
# the tick the last pass's first display lands; see the timeline there.
#
# Writes ONLY transformation.scale, never a transformation compound. The
# compound form decomposes the matrix and resets the other fields, which
# would yank every display back to the anchor as it shrank — the same
# trap documented in the rychess applyTransform notes. The targeted-field
# write preserves the translation the display just flew to, and still
# triggers the interpolation.
#
# vanishTicks controls the shrink. It is set separately first because the
# displays are still carrying interpolationTicks from their flight, which
# would drag the fade out to nearly two seconds. 0 makes them pop.
@using irodoriAnimation
@fast

@for Int i in list::range(0, blockColors.length())
    @bypass /execute in {{animWorld}} run data merge entity @e[limit=1,tag=iro{{i}}] {interpolation_duration:{{vanishTicks}}}
    @bypass /execute in {{animWorld}} run data modify entity @e[limit=1,tag=iro{{i}}] transformation.scale set value [0f,0f,0f]

    @for Int j in list::range(0, stepTicks)
        @delay 1
    @done
@done
